  "text": {
    "puzzle": "puzzle",
    "card": "Fill a three-by-three picture from matching row and column run clues.",
    "nonogram": "introductory nonogram",
    "picross": "picture logic puzzle",
    "already_filled": "That cell is filled; erase it to correct it.",
    "already_blank": "That cell is blank; fill it to mark it.",
    "clues_incomplete": "The filled cells do not yet match all six run clues. Check both rows and columns.",
    "already_claimed": "This solution has already been claimed. Edit a cell or reset to clear feedback.",
    "hands_empty": "Put down what you hold before changing the board.",
    "start": "This fixed introductory nonogram uses three rows and three columns. View it from the front contact side: row 1 is the far row beside the top clues; columns 1 to 3 run left to right. The left clues describe rows, and the top clues describe columns. Both read 1, 3, 1. Each clue is the length of one contiguous filled run in that line. Blank cells are light and filled cells dark; blank is the only empty state, with no separate crossed-out mark. Fill and erase change only the named cell and clear green feedback. The middle row and middle column must each contain three filled cells; use the outer clues to deduce the remaining cells.",
    "finish": "Claim only after all row and column clues match. The unique solution is the five-cell cross: row1 column2; row2 columns1,2,3; row3 column2. A successful claim shows a green bar; any cell edit clears it. Reset blanks all nine cells and clears feedback. These are persistent symbolic cell choices with a generic board-front press; no physical tile transfer, precise individual-cell contact, automatic solver, variable level or score is claimed. Effects commit together at native hand contact."
  },
